{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790463316,"nonce":"AfHnqC5AOIkaEd20hAE2jUbDw+8=","title":"Python: a module function named twice as a value gives two distinct objects","message":"Each mention of a module function as a value builds a new function record (function_value -\u003e zb_func_new), so `f = square; print(square is f, square in [square])` prints `False False` where CPython prints `True True`. Identity, `in` over lists of functions and dict keys by function all see different objects. A per-function record created once (a module slot filled on first use) would give one object per def.","files":null}]}